      "text": "This highlights the challenges of working with real source waters. Therefore, to investigate the effect of a specific source water characteristic, careful control of matrix conditions is necessary to minimize confounding variables. Previous research (Abulikemu et\u00a0al. 2025) established standard- ized water adsorption isotherms for three commonly used bitu- minous coal- based GACs and nine drinking water relevant PFAS through a series of single- solute isotherms (SSI) in buffered Type 1 water (resistivity \u2265 18.2 M\u03a9- cm). The objective of the current study was to evaluate the effect of source water characteristics on the standardized water adsorption isotherms for drinking water relevant concentrations, here using a single GAC. Specifically: (1) to investigate competitive adsorption among coexisting PFAS and its prediction by IAST, and (2) evaluate the effect of background matrix characteristics, including (a) pH and ionic strength, (b) divalent inorganic ions calcium (Ca 2+) and sulfate (SO4 2\u2212), and (c) NOM with and without Ca2+ and SO4 2\u2212 present. These results can help improve how the impact of water matrices on PFAS adsorption is considered in drinking water treatment and provide more context for how these components impact the ability to model such processes. 2 | Materials and Methods 2.1 | Materials"
